  data:  # 27c3
    kind: invariant  # 27c3
    title: "A resolved FIRST-PARTY symbol must never match an external I/O catalogue entry by short name (python/go/js/ts confirmed; rust clean only by path-format accident)"  # 27c3
    status: violated  # 27c3
    priority: 1  # 27c3
    parent:  # 27c3
    tags:  # 27c3
    - io_boundary  # 27c3
    - taint  # 27c3
    - analysis_quality  # 27c3
    - class_item  # 27c3
    - false_positive  # 27c3
    isbefore: []  # 27c3
    duplicate_of: []  # 27c3
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 27c3
    pr_ref:  # 27c3
    description: "FOUND BY APPLYING THE PLAN'S TWO-LANGUAGES-MINIMUM RULE. Python-only work could not have\nsurfaced this: the first Python probe came back CLEAN, and that turned out to be an\nartifact of which NAME was tested rather than a property of the language.\n\nWHAT IS WRONG. A file that defines its OWN function whose short name collides with a\ncatalogued stdlib primitive gets an I/O boundary attributed to it -- with no import of\nthe real module anywhere in the file. Reproduced with a working positive control (the\nREAL stdlib call must still tag) and a neutral control (an unrelated first-party name\nmust never tag) in every language below:\n\n  language     first-party definition        falsely tagged as\n  python       def makedirs(p)               fs_write    os.makedirs\n  go           func MkdirAll(p, m)           fs_write    os.MkdirAll\n  go           func Command(name, arg...)    subprocess  os/exec.Command\n  javascript   function mkdirSync(p)         fs_write    fs.mkdirSync\n  typescript   function mkdirSync(p)         fs_write    fs.mkdirSync\n  rust         fn create_dir_all(p)          -- CLEAN, and for the wrong reason (below)\n\nThe dst in each case RESOLVED to the repo's own symbol -- e.g.\n`go:/abs/path/app.go:3-3:MkdirAll:function`, is_resolved=True, kind `function`. There is\nno ambiguity about what the callee is. The analyzer knows it is first-party and the\nboundary is attributed to it anyway.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN A NORMAL FALSE POSITIVE. `subprocess` is the boundary\nio_boundary.HIGH_RISK_PRIMITIVES flags as *** HIGH RISK *** on the stated invariant that\nlaunching an external program is arbitrary code execution. Here it fires on a function\nthat launches nothing. And a taint SINK is auto-derived from every write-side io_primitive\n(ADR-0017 section 2b), so a false fs_write/subprocess primitive also mints a false taint\nsink, which can manufacture a violated claim.\n\nMECHANISM, read in code and confirmed by instrumenting the lookup inputs.\nio_boundary._extract_module_hint returns None ONLY for a module slot starting with `/`\nor `\\`. Its docstring states the intent correctly -- \"For resolved edges (file paths in\nposition 2), returns None since the path is not a useful module hint\" -- but the\nheuristic implements only the ABSOLUTE case. With module_hint None,\nlookup_with_module skips the module filter entirely and falls through to\ngate_named_entry, whose no-module branch permits a function-kind hit. Confirmed\nidentical inputs across languages: callee='MkdirAll', module_hint=None,\nis_resolved=True, dst_ref=None.\n\nTHIS IS THE PRODUCTION PATH, NOT A FIXTURE ARTIFACT. cli.py:826 does\n`Path(args.path).resolve()`, so every real run carries absolute paths in first-party\ndsts, which is exactly the case the heuristic sends into the ungated branch.\n\nWHY RUST IS CLEAN, AND WHY THAT IS NOT REASSURING. Rust emits a RELATIVE module slot\n(`rust:app.rs:1-1:create_dir_all:function`). A relative path is not caught by the\nstartswith('/') test, so it is returned AS a module hint, `_module_matches(\"std::fs\",\n\"app.rs\")` fails, and the lookup returns None. The right outcome by accident: rust is\nprotected by a path-format difference between analyzers, not by a rule. If rust ever\nemits absolute paths -- or a repo is analysed from a path shape that changes this -- it\nacquires the defect silently.\n\nTWO CORRECTIONS TO THE FIRST PASS OF THIS MEASUREMENT, recorded so neither is repeated:\n  1. The first probe reported \"python: clean, rust: clean\". WRONG, and it was a clean\n     extreme that should have been distrusted. `remove` happens to be in python.yaml's\n     ambiguous_names (34 entries) and `write` in rust's. Those lists are a curated\n     per-name BLOCKLIST, not a structural invariant -- pick a name outside the list\n     (makedirs) and python fails like the rest.\n  2. A probe of `net/http.Client.Do` (assigned vs inline receiver) returned 0 in BOTH\n     arms. Its positive control therefore FAILED, so it measured nothing and no\n     conclusion is drawn from it. Not cited as evidence anywhere.\n\nSEPARATE FINDING FROM THE SAME SESSION, filed here for context but NOT part of this\nitem's statement: go's receiver typing is the MIRROR of python's. Inline\n`exec.Command(...).Run()` types `.Run()` to os/exec correctly (2 boundaries), while the\nassigned `cmd := exec.Command(...); cmd.Run()` emits `go:external:0-0:Run:unresolved`\nuntyped (1 boundary). Python was broken inline and worked assigned; go is the reverse.\nThat is the \"disjoint failure shapes\" the plan predicted, and it means a python-shaped\nfix would not have transferred.\n\nTHE FIX MUST BE ONE PREDICATE IN ONE PLACE, and it must not be a fifth copy. The\nquestion \"is this dst a resolved first-party symbol?\" is currently answered implicitly\nby a path heuristic inside a hint extractor. Candidate shape: a single explicit\npredicate consumed by the boundary lookup, plus a PARITY TEST that enumerates\nddg_build.all_ddg_languages() and asserts, per language, that a first-party definition\nshadowing a catalogued name tags ZERO boundaries while the real stdlib call still tags --\nso the next language fails the test rather than silently skipping the fix.\n\nBLAST RADIUS MUST BE MEASURED BEFORE THE GATE MOVES. LIVE.md records that gating the\nhinted path with gate_named_entry once destroyed 61.5-87.2% of real boundaries for zero\ngain. The measurement to run first is: of all boundary-tagged edges on a real corpus,\nwhat share have a first-party (absolute-path) module slot? That share is both the size\nof the false attribution AND the exposure of any fix that gets the predicate wrong."  # 27c3
    fields:  # 27c3
      statement: "For every language, an edge whose dst resolved to a symbol defined inside the analysed repository must never be tagged with an io_boundary drawn from the external stdlib catalogue, regardless of short-name collision."  # 27c3
      root_cause: "io_boundary._extract_module_hint returns None only for a module slot starting with / or backslash, so a resolved first-party dst carrying an ABSOLUTE path (which cli.py:826 guarantees via Path(args.path).resolve()) yields no module context; lookup_with_module then skips the module filter and falls through to gate_named_entry, whose no-module branch permits a function-kind hit. Rust escapes only because it emits a RELATIVE module slot, which is returned as a hint and fails _module_matches."  # 27c3
