- op: discuss  # bb60
  at: '2026-08-10T02:04:16Z'  # bb60
  by: agent  # bb60
  actor: jgstern_agent  # bb60
  clock: 3  # bb60
  nonce: bb60  # bb60
  message: "SATISFIED in PR #256, on BEHAVIOURAL evidence -- the item's own filed repro re-run per\nlanguage, both directions, after the fix landed:\n\n  language     local definition (must be 0)   real stdlib call (must be >=1)\n  python       makedirs           0            os.makedirs            1\n  go           MkdirAll           0            os.MkdirAll            1\n  javascript   mkdirSync          0            fs.mkdirSync           1\n  rust         create_dir_all     0            std::fs.create_dir_all 1\n\nThe positive column is the part that makes the zeros mean anything: a rule that\nsuppressed everything would also read 0 on the left.\n\nWHAT SHIPPED, and it is narrower than this item first proposed. The refusal withholds\nONLY the ungated short-name fallback inside lookup_with_module -- not the edge, and not\nthe exact-qualified or module-filtered paths.\n\nI GOT THIS WRONG TWICE AND NEITHER CATCH WAS MINE. Recorded because the corrections are\nthe durable content here, not the fix.\n\n  1. REFUSING THE EDGE OUTRIGHT WAS TOO BROAD, and 50 existing tests said so. They model\n     a RESOLVED EXTERNAL primitive as python:/stdlib/os.py:100-102:os.listdir:function --\n     a genuine stdlib call carrying a file-path module slot and a `function` kind. The\n     kind slot cannot separate that from a first-party definition. The module-qualified\n     NAME it still carries can, via the exact-qualified match, which is why the refusal\n     belongs at the short-name fallback and nowhere earlier. The tests were right to\n     break.\n\n  2. KEYING ON THE KIND SLOT ALONE INTRODUCED A FALSE NEGATIVE -- the expensive\n     direction, and precisely the harm this item was filed to prevent. Haskell emits its\n     external placeholders as haskell:external:0-0:readFile:function (module slot\n     `external`, kind `function`), so readFile/writeFile silently stopped tagging. Caught\n     by the full suite. The predicate now requires BOTH a callable kind AND a filesystem\n     module slot, and the haskell shape is pinned as a regression test.\n\nTHE MEASUREMENT THAT PRECEDED THE FIX WAS TRUE AND INSUFFICIENT, which is the lesson\nworth carrying. The corpus scan showed no tagged boundary carrying a `function` kind\nacross hypergumbo / poetry / caddy / express. That is true of those four repos and false\nof haskell -- which was absent from the scan because it is not dataflow-capable, so the\nlanguage enumeration I chose excluded the counterexample. A corpus scan answers a\nquestion about corpora; it does not answer a question about the emission vocabulary.\n\nBLAST RADIUS, measured BEFORE the gate moved (LIVE.md records that gating the hinted path\nonce destroyed 61.5-87.2% of real boundaries for zero gain): every currently-tagged\nboundary on those four repos carries kind unresolved (12,377), attribute (691), variable\n(11) or symbol (3). Not one carries function or method. So this removed zero boundaries\nthat exist today, and self-claims is byte-identical across the change (18 claims, 16\ninconclusive, 2 violated).\n\nTRUE POSITIVES DELIBERATELY PRESERVED: express has 11 tagged boundaries on first-party\ndsts of kind `variable`, reached through `var dirname = path.dirname` in lib/view.js --\nan ALIAS to the real function, where the tag is CORRECT. A blanket first-party refusal\nwould have deleted them. Alias bindings are left alone as a separate, unresolved question\nand pinned by a test so a future widening has to argue with an assertion.\n\nHONEST SCOPE: this is a LATENT hole, not a live epidemic. Zero occurrences on hypergumbo,\npoetry and caddy; the express hits are the alias shape above, not this defect. The\nfixtures prove the mechanism; the corpora say it rarely fires today. It is filed and\nfixed because the mechanism is real and the failure mode is a false HIGH-RISK subprocess\nreport, not because it is currently common.\n\nRESIDUAL, DISCLOSED RATHER THAN CLOSED: a first-party dst carrying a RELATIVE path is not\nrecognised by the predicate. Rust emits that shape and is protected instead by the module\nfilter, where _module_matches(\"std::fs\", \"app.rs\") fails. Two mechanisms covering one\ncase is fragile. Pinned as a passing test with a written re-evaluation trigger: if an\nanalyzer ever emits a relative first-party path AND a module slot that could pass\n_module_matches, the gap becomes reachable and the predicate must widen to a real path\ntest.\n\nPARITY TEST SHIPPED: TestEveryDataflowCapableLanguage enumerates the LIVE DDG registry\n(python, go, javascript, typescript, rust) and asserts both directions per language, so a\nnewly wired language FAILS rather than silently skipping the rule. It forces registration\nthrough production's own helper rather than trusting ambient import order, because that\norder is itself a live defect (WI-kogop) and a parity test that silently enumerates one\nlanguage is worse than none."  # bb60
